      </div>
      <p style="margin: 16px 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 14px">Against other Claude Code guards, on the same 48 MoatBench attack scenarios (2026-10-30): OpenMoat 0.12.0 stopped 39 and left 3 more to the OS sandbox, cc-safety-net 2.7.1 stopped 17, bash-authorize 0.9.9 8 and guardhook 0.2.0 5, none with a false positive on the 8 benign ones. OpenMoat's other 6 are deferred to Claude Code's own prompt by design, as the scenarios expect. The scenarios are OpenMoat's own, so a full score shows that the policy and the benchmark agree, not that OpenMoat is complete. <code style="font-family: 'Geist Mono', monospace; color: var(--fg-2)">openmoat bench</code> runs all 64 (48 attack, 8 benign, 8 developer workflows). <a href="https://github.com/crocodile-labs/openmoat/blob/main/docs/MOATBENCH.md#comparison-with-other-claude-code-guards">Method and limits</a></p>
      <p style="margin: 16px 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 14px">Every valid bypass becomes a test before its fix ships. <a href="/docs/limits/">Read the limits</a> · <a href="https://github.com/crocodile-labs/openmoat/blob/main/docs/EVIDENCE.md">See the test results</a></p>
